“AN ORDINANCE AMENDING SECTION 2.64.090, DEMOLITION PERMIT
REVIEW, OF CHAPTER 2.64, HISTORIC PRESERVATION BOARD, OF TITLE 2,
ADMINISTRATION AND PERSONNEL, OF THE CHEYENNE CITY CODE, PERTAINING
TO APPLICATIONS FOR DEMOLITION AND MOVING PERMITS INVOLVING
CERTAIN BUILDINGS AND STRUCTURES.” John Fafoutakis spoke in support of the
ordinance.  Mr. Collins moved to approve on second reading, seconded by Ms. Broyles.
Comment followed on the application review process by the Historic Preservation Board,
appeals involving the Board of Appeals, and potential expense to property owners.  Upon
inquiry, Erik Rasmussen, Deputy City Attorney, provided information on the appeals process
when a decision rendered by District Court is involved.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body present with the exception of Mr. Pierson and Mayor Spiker
voting “no”.
“AN ORDINANCE DELETING AND RECREATING SECTION 2.04.142 (C),
CONSENT AGENDA, OF TITLE 2, ADMINISTRATION AND PERSONNEL, OF CHAPTER
2.04, MAYOR AND CITY COUNCIL, OF THE CHEYENNE CITY CODE, PERTAINING TO
THE MEETING AGENDA FORMAT FOR REGULAR MEETINGS OF THE GOVERNING
BODY OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE.”  John Fafoutakis spoke in opposition to the ordinance
and referenced equal rights protection contained in the U.S. Constitution. Reporting for Public
Services Committee, Dr. Rinne moved to approve on second reading, seconded by Mr. Collins.
Upon inquiry concerning equal protection afforded under the U.S. Constitution, Erik Rasmussen,
Deputy City Attorney, advised the City Attorney’s office is contracting with an outside attorney
to research the issue, and that further information would be provided at the next Committee
meeting. Comments followed in opposition to procedures outlined in the proposed ordinance;
potential legalities involving equal protection; discussion/debate limitation; Consent Agenda
item removal and approval procedures; constituent(s) representation; other Wyoming
municipalities and organizations that utilize a Consent Agenda process; efficiency of meetings,
and public request to a governing body member(s) to withdraw a Consent Agenda item.  Mr.
Laybourn moved to postpone for two weeks (to Oct. 27, 2008) to allow time for legal analysis of
issues raised, seconded by Mr. Valdez.  Mr. Fafoutakis, Steve Freudenthal, Hu Wang, C. J.
Brown, Beth Rulli, J. Fred Volk, Stephen Karajanis and Paula Qualls spoke in favor of
postponement and in opposition to the ordinance as written.  Topics presented included: U.S.
Constitution and1 amendment rights; alternate options that could be utilized to provide for
st
efficient meetings while affording basic rights to all governing body members; democratic
```

### Page 3 of 11

```text
process; citizen request to remove Consent Agenda items; transparency; concern with issues
involving respect among Council members, and Robert’s Rules of Order.  Mayor Spiker
reviewed the Consent Agenda process, stating the proposed ordinance allows the public an
opportunity to remove an eligible Consent Agenda item for discussion by contacting a Council
member and requesting the item be removed. Discussion followed in support and non-support of
postponement of the ordinance on 2 reading.  During discussion, Mr. Collins raised a point of
nd
order that comments being made were not germane to the motion on the floor with Mayor Spiker
ruling that comments should remain pertinent to the issue of postponement.  Motion to postpone
failed.  Voting “yes” - Mr. Laybourn and Mr. Valdez.  Voting “no” - Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles,
Mr. Collins, Mr. Pierson, Dr. Rinne, Mr. Segrave and Mayor Spiker.  Discussion on the main
motion included the following topics: Streamlining of meetings to benefit the public and staff;
purpose of a Consent Agenda; time length of governing body meetings; concerns and issues
involving the current process utilized to remove Consent Agenda items; public participation;
potential legalities; changes in procedure contained in the proposed ordinance, and
accountability.  Pursuant to comments that the intent of the proposed ordinance was to retain
wording involving audience requests to Council members to withdraw Consent Agenda items
and determination that the proposed ordinance did not include the wording, Mr. Brown requested
City Clerk Carol Intlekofer read current City Code language pertaining to audience requests.
Following reading of the City Code language, Mr. Brown suggested an amendment be
considered. Following a 10 minute recess to review proposed language to be offered as an
amendment and its placement within the body of the ordinance,  Mayor Spiker reconvened the
meeting at 7:59 p.m. Mayor Spiker announced that during the recess Mr. Freudenthal, local
attorney and former state attorney general, had offered his services to review the ordinance and
affiliated issue involving basic rights of governing body members, and propose alternate options
for addressing meeting efficiency and Consent Agenda procedural concerns.  He advised that
Mr. Freudenthal had indicated he could submit a draft proposal at the next Public Services
Committee meeting. Comment was made on procedural concerns.  Mr. Brown moved to call for
the question (to limit debate), seconded by Mr. Segrave.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” - all
members of the governing body present with the exception of Mr. Laybourn and Mr. Valdez
voting “no”.  Main motion carried.  Voting “yes” - all members of the governing body present
with the exception of Mr. Laybourn, Dr. Rinne and Mr. Valdez voting “no”.
“AN ORDINANCE AMENDING SECTION 6.12.020, RABIES TAGS, OF CHAPTER
6.12, DOG AND CAT REGISTRATION, AND SECTION 6.16.020, RABIES VACCINATION
REQUIRED, OF CHAPTER 6.16, RABIES CONTROL, OF TITLE 6, ANIMALS, OF THE
CODE OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE, WYOMING.” Karon Volk spoke in support of the
ordinance change, submitted a packet of information (on file with office of City Clerk)
containing research on rabies vaccines and written statements in support of the ordinance, and
discussed standard guidelines for rabies vaccines, over-vaccinations, medical exemption/waiver
and duration effectiveness of vaccinations.  Also speaking in support of the ordinance were:
Judy Johnstone, John Fafoutakis, Beth Rulli, Emily Sieger, J. Fred Volk and Barb Patterson.
Comments included the following topics: Ordinance change to provide for less frequent animal
vaccinations and providing pet owners with choices; research information; over-vaccination
concerns; potential animal reactions to vaccines; support for professional veterinary animal care
services, and animal registration and vaccination requirements.  Mr. Collins moved to approve
on second reading, seconded by Mr. Pierson.  Mr. Pierson moved to amend by substitute
```

### Page 4 of 11

```text
ordinance, seconded by Mr. Collins.  It was advised the substitute provides for registration tags
if the vaccination is valid for a period longer than one year; animal owner’s discretion on
vaccination time frames, and a medical exemption section.  Ms. Volk spoke in favor of the
proposed substitute.  Comment followed on animal registration requirements.  Pursuant to
inquiry concerning grammatical and clerical errors noticed in the proposed substitute, it was
determined the errors could be corrected by the City Clerk without formal amendment.  Motion
to amend by substitute carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.
Following brief comments, main motion as amended by substitute carried.  Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body present.
“AN ORDINANCE AMENDING SECTIONS 2.04.040, 2.04.160 AND 2.04.170 OF
TITLE 2, ADMINISTRATION AND PERSONNEL, CHAPTER 2.04, MAYOR AND CITY
COUNCIL, OF THE CHEYENNE CITY CODE TO PROVIDE FOR A CLERICAL
