[CA]
“A RESOLUTION AUTHORIZING THE USE OF 2003-2006 1% OPTIONAL SALES
TAX OVERAGE FUNDS IN THE AMOUNT OF $147,280.00 FOR THE PURPOSE OF
REIMBURSING APPROVED EXPENDITURES FROM THE 2007-2010 PARKS AND
RECREATION DEPARTMENTS 1% OPTIONAL SALES TAX ALLOCATION.”  Motion (per
consent agenda) carried.  (#5074)
“A RESOLUTION CERTIFYING THAT THE GOVERNING BODY OF THE CITY
OF CHEYENNE IS IN AGREEMENT WITH THE LARAMIE COUNTY BOARD OF
COMMISSIONERS AS TO THE 2009 COUNTYWIDE CONSENSUS BLOCK GRANT
APPLICATION TO THE STATE LOAN AND INVESTMENT BOARD.”  Frank Cole,
developer; Mark Shubert, and Rick Fierro, Ram Trucking, Inc., spoke in opposition to the
proposed SLIB funding request relative to the Construction and Demolition Recycling project.
Comments included the following topics: City competition with private businesses; free
enterprise; utilization of funding for other projects; community health, safety and welfare needs;
contracting with private business provider(s) for services vs. the City operating the recycling
program; construction debris vs. household waste issues; program efficiency; recycling market
opportunities; City’s compost facility, and diverting materials from the City’s landfill.  Upon
inquiry, Mike Abel, Public Works Director, provided information on solid waste landfill
diversion; construction/demolition debris; household waste and recycling program goal, and
Federal permitting regulations.  Dennis Pino, Solid Waste Director, provided information on
current compost facility operations; reviewed the Department’s waste diversion project,
commenting staff has been working on this for 10 years, and discussed contaminated materials
and DEQ requirements.  He stated the overall goal is to extend the life of the City’s landfill and
advised the Department has been accepting construction and demolition debris since the
inception of the landfill, explaining material recycling efforts.  Further comments by Mr. Fierro
and Mr. Shubert included City competition in the area of roll-off containers; Sanitation’s
disposal, roll-off container rental and overage fees, and support for private enterprise.  Upon
inquiry from Donn Edmunds concerning the total amount of block grant funds requested for all
county-wide projects, Mayor Spiker advised the estimated total amount is $20,000,000.00 with
```

### Page 5 of 11

```text
the City’s portion being approximately $16,000,000.00.  Mr. Edmunds commented on publicly
vs. privately owned and operated parking lots in the downtown area, Henderson draw project and
Taco John’s Events Center elevator project.  Mayor Spiker clarified the elevator at the Events
Center was inspected when the building was initially purchased by the City, and inspectors had
advised the elevator needed to be upgraded. Upon inquiry, City Engineer Ken Lewis reviewed
the Deming/Waltersheid project included on the list.  Mr. Cole commented on Sanitation roll-off
container rates, and Hu Wang stated the City needs to conduct research and plan for the future.
Mr. Segrave moved to adopt, seconded by Mr. Brown.  Comments included the following topics:
assignment of agenda items to Council standing committees; compilation of proposed projects;
private business competition concerns; single stream recycling; transporting of certain waste
materials out of town, and projects involving a new downtown parking lot and Civic Center
“rigging”.  During comments and upon inquiry, Mr. Pino provided information on the curbside
recycling pilot program, and transporting of certain wastes to Colorado.  Tim Thorson, Laramie
County Grants Manager, provided information on state rules governing the distribution of SLIB
money and qualifying projects, and explained timing issues involving the rules, identification of
qualifying projects and the City’s budget time frame.  Mayor Spiker advised acquisition of
property for the  Downtown Parking Lot-East project is contingent upon due diligence being
conducted, including a property appraisal; governing body approval, and SLIB Board approval,
and provided information on use of the proposed funding for the parking lot project.  Mr.
Laybourn moved to amend (the project list) by striking the Civic Center Power Assisted Rigging
project and the Downtown Parking Lot-East project, and moving the funding proposed for these
projects to the prioritized substitute list to assist with annexation of County pockets (water and
sewer costs).  Motion died due to a lack of a second.  Following a 10 minute recess, Mayor
Spiker reconvened the meeting at 10:00 p.m. Upon inquiry, Mr. Thorson provided information
on the proposed Laramie County morgue project.  Discussion followed regarding the
Construction and Demolition Recycling project; permitting requirements through DEQ and staff
research that had been conducted indicating there was no community private business that had
yet been approved (permitted) through DEQ to handle the volume of construction debris/waste.
Mr. Abel advised solid waste future options have been reviewed and researched and explained
partnerships have been developed with private sector firms in some areas.  He discussed
construction debris volumes, waste diversion, proposed project facility to be built at the landfill,
efficiency, goal of community-wide recycling, and the importance of meeting DEQ permitting
requirements, and stated that program partners will be looked at to help the City.  Further
comments were made relative to the proposed downtown parking lot, and construction cost
increases affecting the Norris Viaduct and Walterscheid projects.  Upon inquiry, Mr. Fierro
advised he is pursuing acquisition of a permit through DEQ and was currently operating under a
low hazard, low volume exemption, and commented on DEQ’s extensive permitting process.
Discussion followed on Sanitation revenues and the general fund, and the proposed parking lot
project, identified by the Downtown Development Authority as a priority in providing assistance
with downtown revitalization efforts.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all  members of the
governing body present with Mr. Laybourn voting “no”.  (#5075)
